00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.680
So Aviatrix is a company
based in Silicon Valley.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.220
Our mission is to
make cloud networking

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.250
as simple and dynamic
as compute and storage.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.330
With Aviatrix, the
cloud networking

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.960
become a substrate
in your full stack,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.930
and we take care of
that, so that you

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.860
can focus on building awesome
applications and services.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.770
So how do we do that?

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.250
Traditionally, you know
how networking devices, the

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.140
approaches you deploy.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.760
It's very much hardware-based.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.240
You deploy the device and
you configure and deploy

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:19.780
another one.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.190
You configure it.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.850
We take a totally
different approach.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.060
In our approach,
there is a controller.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.080
It's a software, more
defined approach, where

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.850
you started with a controller.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.240
The controller is deployed
either on prem or in the cloud.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.830
And the controller is given
credentials, the cloud

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:45.940
credentials, so that it can
use APIs to launch gateways

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.410
in the cloud in the
network of your choice.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.170
And from there on, you
can build IPSec tunnels,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.340
or you can build SSL/VPNs
through all point-and-click

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.590
or REST API-driven.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:04.550
So if you think about
cloud networking,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.250
it really has all three parts.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.740
Either you're talking about
connecting from on prem

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.960
to the cloud, or you're
talking about connecting

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.970
from cloud to cloud,
or talking about user

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.500
connecting to the cloud.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.040
And we have the product
for all three capabilities,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.720
for all three, for all
the major cloud providers.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:29.000
And in addition to that,
in terms of IPSec VPN,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.060
we also have scale-out
solution where

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.120
you can deploy a cluster
of gateways in case

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.150
you want to have a
communication of throughput

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.790
that more than a single
instance based performance.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:48.370
And today, I'm going to focus
on one of the capabilities we

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.650
have, which is a user VPN.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.310
It's SSL based.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.180
Now, if you are a
enterprise customer,

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.960
you probably wonder,
why do I need SSL/VPN?

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.700
I already have that
for my corporate IT,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.280
and then I connect
it to the cloud.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.680
But you'd be surprised
how our customers use

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.920
our device, this capability.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.660
With the cloud, what you can do
is let's say you have partners

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.370
and you have contractors.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:19.329
Traditionally, you bring
them on prem to your DMZ,

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.150
and that create a
lot of security risk

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.050
and a lot of complications.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.040
With the cloud, you can actually
set up a cloud network project

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.530
account for them, and you
meet them in the cloud

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.170
and you give them
certain permissions,

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.700
but you have complete
control to it.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.060
And that's one of the
very popular case,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:45.960
even for enterprise to
use these capability.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.510
Now, you say, if I'm a start-up,
why do I care about SSL/VPN?

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.460
Of course, if you, let's say,
at the most basic challenges

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.790
for user access, is let's
say you have nothing on prem,

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.400
everything is in
the cloud, then you

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.460
need to have your
developers accessing.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.810
Now, without any VPN capability,
you either deploy everything

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.600
in a public subnet, which
means you invite attacks

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.220
on your public-facing
instances, or you

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.670
do some kind of jump
bastion station or jump

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.460
host where you log
in to one instance

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.070
and get to the other one.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.520
In either case, the key
management and secure access

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.010
become a challenge, even
for start-up companies,

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.170
because most of your
instances, your database,

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.100
your application, should
be put in private.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.040
They should not have
public-facing access.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:50.710
So what's a unique thing about
Aviatrix's SSL/VPN solution?

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:58.720
We build ground-up for the
cloud deployment in the--

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.640
for example, let's
say the SSL/VPN,

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.020
how do you manage
multi-factor authentication?

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:08.980
We integrate with the
dual [INAUDIBLE] LDAP,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.880
and we are the only
open VPN-based provider

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.430
that supports SAML
integration, so that you

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:18.880
can integrate with your own
enterprise identity management

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.630
and have single
sign-on into the cloud.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:26.270
And so how do we
load balancing VPN,

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.890
if I cannot have a single
instance, and if that fail,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:29.560
what do I do?

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.160
Because most of virtualized
appliance put in the cloud

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.560
is a single instance based.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.890
We provide a Google-native
load balancer

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.910
based VPN solution where you
can scale out to thousands

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.580
of tens of thousands of users.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.125
The largest internet streaming
company use our product

00:06:48.125 --> 00:06:51.100
for the VPN access.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.200
So that's scaling out.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.230
How do we manage users?

00:06:56.230 --> 00:07:00.520
We have this called
profile-based access,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.830
where you can assign users--

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.790
your contractors, your partners,
your DevOps, your employees--

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:12.240
and you can give security
policies when they connect to,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.340
and it will push down to the
gateway where they landed,

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:21.220
and it will stop them from
accessing their permitted

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.380
resources at the gateway level.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.780
So there is a profile based.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:27.310
And then how do we do logging?

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:28.720
How do we do logging?

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.360
Again, we integrate with
all the popular-- we

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.560
don't want to have another
dashboard to look at events.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.330
We integrate with the Splunk,
Sumo Logic, Logstash, Datadog,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.330
so that you can have complete
visibility to all the TCP

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:43.870
sessions.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.640
All the network
events are completely

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:49.660
logged to your
central log-in system.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.020
And another powerful
thing about this solution

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:58.270
is because we do also IPSec
VPN, so you can integrate it

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.330
with that, meaning
that when a user landed

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:05.280
into cloud, together
with the VPN access,

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.230
they can actually
access any cloud

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.080
network, any instances
with the profile-based,

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.750
with the multi-factor
authentication,

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.810
and with all logging
capability built in.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:18.340
So that's kind of--

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:23.980
this is the solution we provide.

