00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:37.100
at Brocade.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.490
So what I'm going to talk to
you in the next 10 minutes or so

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:46.490
is about our ADC and WAF
solutions on Google Cloud.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:49.130
Before I tell you
the why, let me

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:50.780
rewind a little
bit, a few years,

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.870
and show you how load balancing
deployments have changed.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.910
I'm sure my
colleagues here would

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:00.390
agree to how that is happening.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.960
Enterprises used to deploy
a large load balancer

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.110
in their data center and then
hang several dozen applications

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:07.830
off it.

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:09.930
What we're seeing now with
cloud-based deployments

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:13.240
is that the mode of deployment
is slightly changing.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.196
So we're having more number of
load balancers being deployed,

00:12:16.196 --> 00:12:17.820
but lesser number of
applications being

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.080
hung off each load balancer.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.029
What that really means
is the deployment,

00:12:23.029 --> 00:12:24.570
the number of
deployments are clearly

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.150
increasing, because you have
more number of those instances

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:28.260
being deployed.

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:30.889
But at the same time, the
lifecycle for those deployments

00:12:30.889 --> 00:12:31.680
were also changing.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.138
So you may want to
spin up and down

00:12:33.138 --> 00:12:37.410
an instance at short notice and
service a set of application

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:40.830
workloads that are in the cloud
at a certain point in time,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.740
necessarily available for a
user at a certain point in time,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:44.560
or not.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.500
So that's one of the
trends that we're seeing.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:50.636
And with the Brocade virtual
ADC solution, first of all,

00:12:50.636 --> 00:12:52.010
it's purpose-built
for the cloud.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:56.220
So we never sold, you know, the
virtual ADC as a hardware box.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.080
It was built with cloud
and virtualization

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.290
in mind from the very inception.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:05.160
And that's why it's optimized
for any cloud anywhere

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.800
kind of an environment.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.330
The ease of
programming, the ease

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.060
of flexibility of applying
business logic on the box,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.310
is greatly simplified
by the scripting

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.770
tools we offer on the system.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.680
And most importantly,
the ability

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.860
to have customized licensing,
or flexible licensing,

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.160
plays a role here.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:25.650
Now again, if you
go back to what

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:29.250
I mentioned about
instances being spun

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.522
up and down at short
notice, the piece

00:13:32.522 --> 00:13:34.230
that would stick out
there is, how do you

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:36.430
license those instances
at short notice as well?

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:39.060
So you can't really
have a static tie-in

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:42.810
of the license
with that instance

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:44.730
and then keep that
available for very long,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:45.910
because you don't
need that instance.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.368
You might want to
reuse the license

00:13:47.368 --> 00:13:50.080
at a different deployment
at a separate point in time.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.500
So that's where our
flexible licensing

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:55.740
model comes into play, and it's
truly unique across the market.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:03.022
So a few key things on where
we continue to add value.

00:14:03.022 --> 00:14:04.730
So let's take health
checks, for example.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.220
So load balancing is
clearly a solution

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:10.040
where you want to monitor
the health of your appliances

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:14.060
or the back-end nodes and
only load balance traffic

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:15.185
if the node is actually up.

00:14:15.185 --> 00:14:17.184
Well, what you can do
with Brocade's solution is

00:14:17.184 --> 00:14:18.760
you can do not
just one hop away,

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:20.010
you can actually do multi-hop.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:23.330
So for example, if a web server
is connected to a database

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:26.300
and your ability to
load balance really

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.460
depends on the availability
of the database,

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.780
you can do the multi-hop
kind of a situation.

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:33.260
So you don't load
balance to a web server

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:35.109
if the database tied
to it is actually down,

00:14:35.109 --> 00:14:36.650
because it doesn't
make sense, right?

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:39.250
I mean, you may need
information from the database

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:41.960
when it's actually
not available.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:45.210
The other key piece here
is speed and reliability.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:49.910
So we've measured one of the
highest throughput measurements

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:54.620
in the industry for a virtual
solution on a single instance.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:57.500
We go up to upwards
of 140 gigabits

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:00.230
per second of throughput on
a single virtual instance.

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:02.460
Now, if you think about
certain use cases,

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.400
like object storage,
where a lot of traffic

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:06.180
is being pumped through
a load balancer,

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:08.340
these become
critically important,

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:11.722
especially when you have a
single instance funneling

00:15:11.722 --> 00:15:12.430
a lot of traffic.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:18.730
I talked about scripting as
an important differentiator

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:19.660
for what we have.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:22.810
Now that, again, translates
to if you can describe

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:26.440
your problem, how do you apply
request and response rules

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.680
on your load balancer
such that it's

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:31.300
able to translate
the business logic

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.540
into actual
decision-making rules?

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:38.085
So what we have
built in is a GUI.

00:15:38.085 --> 00:15:40.210
So let's say your problem
that you want to solve is

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.610
if you're load balancing to
an e-commerce credit card

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:48.730
processing node, unless
the response time is--

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:51.547
if the response time happens to
be greater than x milliseconds,

00:15:51.547 --> 00:15:53.380
then you don't want to
balance to that node,

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.390
because clearly
something might be wrong.

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:57.940
Maybe the credit card processing
engine is not working.

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:01.120
You may want to, at that point,
redirect your credit card

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:04.300
request to a different
web server that

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:08.050
can process that transaction.

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:10.870
So what TrafficScript
would do is essentially

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:12.430
look for a response
from the node

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:14.110
and take an intelligent
decision based

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:15.820
on what kind of
business logic you want

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.290
to apply for that situation.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.920
So if your logic dictates that
if the transaction time is

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:22.600
greater than x
milliseconds, then please

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:25.450
redirect me to a node
where things are actually

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:26.590
processed faster.

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:28.870
From an end user
perspective, all you see

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:32.494
is maybe the little wheels
spin a few milliseconds extra,

00:16:32.494 --> 00:16:33.910
but your transaction
has processed

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:36.610
much faster because of the
request and response rules

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:39.460
that you've applied.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.850
So let me talk a little
bit about the pieces

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:43.300
within the portfolio.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.364
What we have is the
traffic manager, which

00:16:45.364 --> 00:16:46.530
is the load balancing piece.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:49.110
We have a web application
firewall, which does

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.260
[INAUDIBLE], top 10, you
know, attack detection.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:55.649
It resides in the same module
as the traffic manager.

00:16:55.649 --> 00:16:57.690
So as long as you can
deploy the traffic manager,

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:59.785
you get web application
protection as well.

00:16:59.785 --> 00:17:01.410
And then we have the
services director,

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.920
which is responsible for
licensing, orchestration,

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.319
management of these instances.

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:08.220
Again, going back
to my first point

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:12.720
on the importance of being
able to manage the ADC

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.109
estate, if you will, especially
in a cloud environment,

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:16.690
is critically important.

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.200
So together with these
three components,

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:23.530
it forms the virtual
ADC family that we have.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.411
So let me talk about two use
cases that are relevant here.

00:17:26.411 --> 00:17:27.660
The first one is hybrid cloud.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.090
Now clearly, if you're
born in the cloud,

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.790
you have a certain set of
options for the load balancing

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:34.200
solution that you choose.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.920
If you are a traditional, if
you were on prem to start with,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.050
you have a certain
set of solutions.

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:41.790
But a lot of the
enterprises we see

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.204
are in the lift and shift phase.

00:17:43.204 --> 00:17:45.120
So as you're moving your
application workloads

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.730
from on prem to
cloud, the ability

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:50.760
to load balance across the
moving set of workloads

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:51.877
becomes relevant as well.

00:17:51.877 --> 00:17:53.460
So I think that's
one of the key areas

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:56.340
that we see which
is growing, and we

00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:00.640
find that most of the
enterprises are in both places,

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:01.140
right?

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:04.230
So that's where we're seeing
the application workloads

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.840
shift, and the ability
for us to load balance

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.080
there becomes important.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.930
The other one is, again,
proactive security control.

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:17.330
So as you deploy your
applications in the cloud,

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:20.360
what kind of security controls
can you exercise from a layer 7

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:20.870
perspective?

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:23.210
So clearly, your
layer 3 firewall

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:27.350
can only protect what it
can, but it can't really

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:33.030
do things like URL protection
on the URL and things like that,

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:34.610
which are more
application-centric,

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:36.170
and you may need
additional controls

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.120
on the application
side, which actually

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:39.740
prevent layer 7 attacks.

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:41.420
So that's where
the WAF comes in.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:45.020
Again, on Google Cloud, we
have both the virtual traffic

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:48.410
manager and the WAF available
through the launcher, so you

00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:51.740
can obviously just pick
one of them and then--

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:56.510
or both, the combination,
and then launch through GCP.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:58.880
So I wanted to kind of
emphasize the impact

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:00.710
we've had with GCP presence.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:03.920
So we launched this
back sometime last year,

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:06.470
and we've seen tremendous
growth in the amount of usage

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.789
across WAF and
our load balancing

00:19:08.789 --> 00:19:09.830
solution in Google Cloud.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:12.310
So I think that clearly
goes to show, first of all,

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:16.820
there's a lot of interest in
the foundation and the cloud

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:18.680
environment that
Google has set up.

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:21.050
And secondly, there is
a tremendous opportunity

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:24.530
that's ahead of us as well, in
terms of applications moving

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:27.290
to Google, and at the
same time, the network

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:29.870
elements and the solutions
that support application

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:33.780
movement need to be
carefully assessed as well.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:38.609
So that's all I had
today, and thank you.

