cloud since 2008, and up

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:03.000
to this day with all
major cloud providers.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.190
And the use case I
chose to talk to you

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.900
about is protecting
your web servers.

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:11.940
So customer moving
to the cloud, wanting

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:13.910
to put his workloads
on the cloud,

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:16.140
enjoy the electricity
and scalability

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:19.870
of his web presence.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:24.090
So of course, he would need
to run Google instances

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:28.230
on the cloud that will scale
up and down as he needs.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:29.730
In this example,
I chose a customer

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:32.490
who decided to keep his
databases on premise.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:34.680
So he have his
on-premise presence

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.360
where his database are.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:40.860
And this customer also want
to allow his remote workers

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:47.080
to do remote maintenance
on his cloud web servers.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:48.750
So this is kind
of the environment

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:49.864
I want to talk about.

00:21:49.864 --> 00:21:52.155
I'm going to talk about
challenges of this environment.

00:21:52.155 --> 00:21:53.710
There are several.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.790
First of all, Google allow--

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:00.000
any cloud provider allow you
to segment your environment.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.130
That's very, very important.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:03.110
But that's the basics.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:05.640
So you want to segment your
production from your dev,

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:06.360
from your test.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.240
That's needed, and
that's a basic.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:11.380
But segmentation is not enough.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:14.760
You need to be able to
look into the transaction,

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.850
to look into the web request, to
look what is going from the web

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.830
to the application
to the database,

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:23.220
and be able to say whether this
is a legitimate transaction

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.670
or it is a malware trying
to propagate, because today,

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:30.540
you cannot segment at the
actual virtual machine level,

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:34.050
because everything is connected,
everything is hyperconnected,

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:37.870
web to application to database
to web servers to CRM,

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:38.370
et cetera.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:40.980
This is what the
business today requires.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:44.220
So one of the things you
need to be careful about

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.010
is having a server
in your environment

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:49.950
infected and that
infection spreading

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:51.990
within your environment.

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:55.080
So it's enough to have
one server infected,

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:56.730
if you don't have
the right means

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:00.270
to get your entire segment,
your entire environment,

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:01.800
infected with malware.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:05.597
So this is kind of one
of the main concerns

00:23:05.597 --> 00:23:06.430
of this environment.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:12.390
The other is, how do I connect
this threaded data center which

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:15.720
is on premise and off
premise in the cloud,

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:18.310
how do I connect
it in a secure way?

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:22.860
I want to allow my remote
employees to connect securely

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:27.210
to the web servers, and I want
the web servers to be able

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:29.370
to connect securely
to the database,

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:35.430
because I want to be able to
avoid man-in-the-middle attacks

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:40.390
or someone sniffing and
looking at my private data.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:44.680
So this is concern, the second
concern I wanted to talk about.

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:49.440
So this is exactly what
Check Point with Google

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:53.520
work together in order
to provide customers.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:58.490
This product name, our product
family name, is called vSEC.

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:02.790
This is our Check Point
solution for cloud security.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:07.530
And we are announcing today a
Check Point vSEC 4 Google Cloud

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:09.840
Platform.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:14.340
And I'm going to show you how
vSEC allows you to actually

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.770
answer those challenges.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.990
But before we move on,
I do want to mention

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:22.710
that vSEC is a
cross-cloud solution,

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:25.520
so we're supporting all the
major cloud vendors today,

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:29.760
starting from on-premise
VMware software-defined network

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.310
and Cisco and AWS and
Azure and OpenStack

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:35.070
and [INAUDIBLE]
and Alibaba Cloud

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:38.130
and now Google, which is
very strategic and important

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:41.480
step for Check Point.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:45.900
And vSEC allow you to
have a persistent security

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:51.300
policy managing your assets
from different clouds.

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:55.490
So if you have a multi-cloud,
you're using GCP and AWS

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:58.430
and you have on-premise
VMWare and GCP,

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:01.590
you have one security
policy managing everything,

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:06.060
including your physical
on-premise devices.

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:08.450
So let's go back to the
scenario we've discussed.

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:11.180
So we have our web
apps, and now let's

00:25:11.180 --> 00:25:17.360
say we add vSEC, deploying
this within Google Cloud.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:22.070
So first of all, we need to
care about attacks originating

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:25.610
from external, because
a virtual machine

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:31.030
in the cloud with public IP
is under constant attack.

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:33.920
And the attackers need
to succeed only once,

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:37.680
and you guys need to be able
to stop them 100% of the time.

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:40.430
So first and foremost, we
need to inspect the packets,

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:41.990
we need to inspect
the transaction,

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:44.360
to understand whether
there is some threat

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:45.340
on those transactions.

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.055
So vSEC protect you
from external threats.

00:25:48.055 --> 00:25:51.440
This is north-south security,
from external to your data

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:52.850
center in the cloud.

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:57.500
And vSEC allow you to stop
malware before it arrives.

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.080
So if the malware arrive,
vSEC will identify, block it,

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.240
and log the event.

00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:04.550
More than that, if
we think forward,

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:07.520
we talked about the fact
that even if somehow a server

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:10.280
got infected, we don't
want this to spread around.

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:12.860
So vSEC provides you
east-west security

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:15.870
within the environment,
between the data center,

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:20.210
so malware is not able
to propagate laterally

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:21.980
moving within the environment.

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:24.670
This is east-west
threat prevention.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:29.660
Our remote workers can now use
SSL/VPN with the same device

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:32.450
in order to have
encrypted connections

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:34.160
to the environment,
and of course,

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:38.780
site-to-site VPN covering our
web-to-database transaction.

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:40.820
All of this with
a single solution.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:41.960
It's the same solution.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.390
It's the same engine.

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:46.100
A few more points about this.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:48.650
First of all, this is
unified management.

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.330
You have one consistent policy.

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:54.980
You're able to use Google
objects within your security

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:59.570
policy, and whenever anything
changes, IP changes, et cetera,

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:00.830
this automatically adapt.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:03.760
No manual operation
you need to do.

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:05.930
This is very, very
strong around automation.

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:07.850
So it's one-click provisioning.

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:10.280
It scales automatically
as you need,

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:14.900
so you can scale linearly as
you need more security power.

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:17.120
And it provides auto-protection.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.021
Your virtual machine is there.

00:27:19.021 --> 00:27:20.270
You don't need to do anything.

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:23.360
It will be protected
automatically.

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:26.720
It's open, in terms everything
is API-based and CLI-based.

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:28.340
Very strong in terms
of performance.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:31.700
We have over five
gigs of throughput

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:34.490
of performance per
VM with four cores,

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:38.030
and you can adjust
it as you like.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.450
And we are partnering
with VMWare very strongly

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:44.600
on the R&amp;D and product side,
so we're supporting many beta

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:48.950
