00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:27.710
So in terms of
technical concepts,

00:37:27.710 --> 00:37:30.190
let's go little bit
backwards and look at,

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:32.560
what is the public cloud
security architecture?

00:37:32.560 --> 00:37:34.520
It's a shared security model.

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:37.120
Google takes care of the
security of the platform,

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:39.880
and they've got a lot of
amazing technologies and tools

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:42.210
that, for example, Erez
mentioned a few of them.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:45.167
There's IAM, there's
basic firewall rules,

00:37:45.167 --> 00:37:46.750
and there's all this
physical security

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:48.910
protection of the cloud itself.

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:51.760
But once a packet hits
the VPC or the network

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:55.000
that you deployed, you are
responsible for it's security.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:58.360
You can use all the tools,
like IAM, Google Cloud firewall

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:00.220
rules, all those elements.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.110
But you still have
to do that work,

00:38:02.110 --> 00:38:04.570
and you have to make sure
you configure them correctly,

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:06.280
follow all the best
practices, which

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:08.320
is sort of normally
what you would do.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:11.080
So now, in this
environment, what

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:12.910
is the specific
difference or the value

00:38:12.910 --> 00:38:15.040
add of a next-gen firewall?

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:19.620
So typically, if you
look at an attack that

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.380
happens, if you were looking
to prevent an attack,

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:24.450
you first want
complete visibility.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:26.030
Whether it's on prem
or in the cloud,

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:28.050
you need visibility so
that you are actually

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.840
able to see the traffic and
decide if there is actually

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:31.825
a threat or not.

00:38:31.825 --> 00:38:33.450
The second element
you would have to do

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:36.030
is also reduce the
attack surface.

00:38:36.030 --> 00:38:38.077
So for example, if
it's a web application,

00:38:38.077 --> 00:38:40.410
you may configure the basic
Google Cloud firewall rules,

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:43.950
and say, allow port
80 and 443 only.

00:38:43.950 --> 00:38:46.537
Good security best practices.

00:38:46.537 --> 00:38:48.120
If it's a specific
application, you're

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:50.328
going to only allow those
application traffic streams

00:38:50.328 --> 00:38:53.160
to go through that solution.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:57.180
And then comes the real part,
is that any application that

00:38:57.180 --> 00:39:01.360
is out there is where
the attackers are looking

00:39:01.360 --> 00:39:02.920
to exploit vulnerabilities.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:05.350
Anything you hear about
exploits, malware,

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:07.510
all they're trying to do
is exploit vulnerabilities

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:08.740
in the application.

00:39:08.740 --> 00:39:11.904
So you want to know
all the known threats.

00:39:11.904 --> 00:39:13.570
These are the CVs or
the vulnerabilities

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:16.120
that are posted, and then
you're trying to prevent them.

00:39:16.120 --> 00:39:17.500
That's step number one.

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:19.420
That, you got to do
basically day in,

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:21.940
day out, prevent
known vulnerabilities.

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:24.280
Next comes the more
interesting, tricky part.

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:27.100
When new vulnerabilities
are found by researchers

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:29.710
or by attackers,
how quickly do you

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:33.190
turn around and make them
sort of known vulnerabilities

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:36.040
and quickly start protecting
as soon as you can?

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.530
So these can be zero-day
attacks, or even

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:41.710
brand new variations
of known attacks.

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:46.040
So to do this, there's a lot of
things that go under the hood

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:48.140
to make this possible.

00:39:48.140 --> 00:39:50.600
So on the visibility front,
you see all the things

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:52.250
that you have to do.

00:39:52.250 --> 00:39:56.400
To reduce the attack surface,
there's all these other things

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:56.900
to do.

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:59.180
So I'm not going to go
through all the details,

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:00.987
but let's take an example.

00:40:00.987 --> 00:40:02.570
So for example, if
there is a malware,

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.630
it's trying to exploit
a specific application.

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:09.720
Let's say, some bug in NGINX
or some coding issue in NGINX,

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:12.300
the malware is trying
to exploit that.

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:14.467
What they're probably
going to try to do is,

00:40:14.467 --> 00:40:16.050
what are all the
relevant applications

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:18.090
that need to be
controlled, where

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:20.680
this malware is impacting it?

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:22.270
You also want to
potentially look at,

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:24.680
from the visibility
perspective, is that,

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:26.350
is the traffic
actually encrypted,

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:29.560
and pretty much 70%, 80%
or more of the traffic

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:31.780
is now going towards
encrypted traffic.

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:35.140
So potentially, the solution
that protects you also

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:37.660
needs to do SSL
decryption, because you

00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:39.861
have to actually look
at the encrypted traffic

00:40:39.861 --> 00:40:41.860
to see if there's actually
malware going through

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:43.900
or it's benign traffic.

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:45.610
So there's a lot
of elements that

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:50.190
are needed to sort of provide
that full application stack

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:51.600
security.

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:54.215
And this is really hard to do.

00:40:54.215 --> 00:40:55.590
I've got all these
bullet points,

00:40:55.590 --> 00:40:58.440
but there's a lot of components
that go underneath it.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.780
And the last part that
I'm not talking fully

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:05.040
is that beyond just cloud and
on prem, that unknown prevention

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:05.640
part.

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:08.700
That's where a lot of the secret
sauce comes in these days--

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:11.280
applying dynamic
analysis, static analysis,

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.950
applying machine learning
to say what is benign,

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:15.840
what is malicious?

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:17.340
So there's all these
components that

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:20.610
are needed to truly
secure an environment.

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:24.340
So let me give you the
example in a different way.

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:27.720
So if you configure the
base security that's

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.990
available with the
firewall rules,

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:33.640
this is a 5-tuple
rule available.

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:35.430
So it'll say,
source destination,

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:37.260
allow this protocol port.

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.870
That's how you
would configure it.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:41.700
Obviously, there's
a lot more that

00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:43.620
goes into it from that
visibility perspective

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:45.030
that I was talking about.

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:47.700
It's not just that it's a
344-kilobyte transaction.

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:52.500
It's actually an SSL traffic
that is carrying HTTP packets.

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.140
So this is your
application, which is HTTP.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:57.480
What if you really
needed to know more

00:41:57.480 --> 00:42:00.090
to make a decision on
whether to allow it?

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:03.230
What would be ideal is that if
you know what user is involved

00:42:03.230 --> 00:42:06.960
and where is the actual
traffic going to?

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:08.970
So you know the IP,
but you'd rather

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.390
like to know the geolocation, if
it's relevant to your use case.

00:42:12.390 --> 00:42:17.460
You may want to block traffic
going to certain countries.

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:20.140
And specifically, what is the
application going through?

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:21.690
So if you look at
the bottom line,

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:23.040
that's the simple 5-tuple rule.

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.200
Imagine this was
an outbound flow

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:27.240
from your environment
that's going out

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:29.130
to some location in
Canada, essentially,

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:30.780
as I'm showing here.

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.490
What if, instead of
this benign flow,

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:35.100
where a PDF file
is going through,

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:38.440
instead with all
those same details,

00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:40.680
but actually instead
of that PDF file,

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:44.347
it's actually a pre-executable
with some malware inside it.

00:42:44.347 --> 00:42:45.930
There's no way you
would know that you

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:48.150
need to block this
or allow this,

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.570
because the basic port protocol
stuff, the bottom rules,

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:53.452
will allow this.

00:42:53.452 --> 00:42:55.410
So this is what you need
to sort of essentially

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.330
go ahead and protect.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:42:59.670
So to do this, we're
obviously working

00:42:59.670 --> 00:43:02.100
to bring the next-generation
firewall to the Google Cloud

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:03.030
Platform.

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:04.890
And the product is
called VM-Series.

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:07.752
It runs the same PAN-OS
code, the operating system,

00:43:07.752 --> 00:43:09.960
and all the security features
that our customers have

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:12.690
used on prem, and now
it's going to be available

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:15.310
when we launch in the
Google Cloud environment.

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:19.380
So I've got a simple demo
that's in our trade show--

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.050
sorry, on our floor booth,
so you can come take a look.

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:23.640
I'm not running
through the demo here.

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.130
But essentially, the idea is
that now that Google Cloud

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.200
supports a true
multi-NIC environment,

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:29.700
it's going to allow
you to do really

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:31.410
good, clean segmentation.

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:33.390
For example, I've
got a firewall here

00:43:33.390 --> 00:43:35.810
which has a connection in
the management network,

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:37.560
so that you can segment
management network

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:39.270
from your on-prem world.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.700
Then, you can have a
public-facing interface

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:44.220
so that all your public-facing
traffic can come in.

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:46.500
You apply all the policies
that you would like,

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.400
and then you decide whether
it goes to the private network

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:51.900
where your applications
and services are running.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:53.700
Now, on that private
network site,

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:56.610
you can also do segmentation
between application tiers

00:43:56.610 --> 00:44:00.990
to say web server can
talk to database over SQL.

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:03.510
So things like that, all
those advanced logic and rules

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:05.250
can be applied so
that you really

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:08.730
make sure that inbound,
outbound, east-west flows can

00:44:08.730 --> 00:44:12.420
be protected using those rules
that I was showing earlier.

00:44:12.420 --> 00:44:15.090
Now, obviously the example
I showed a second ago

00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:17.010
is kind of a simple layout.

00:44:17.010 --> 00:44:18.900
You really are not going
to deploy at scale.

00:44:18.900 --> 00:44:21.180
The cloud's all
about elasticity.

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:23.700
And the cloud
architecture really

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.920
is about scale out,
which means you

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:29.250
need to leverage the
cloud-native load balancers

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:32.280
and scale out behind
it to meet the demand.

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:34.099
So for example, I've
got this deployment

00:44:34.099 --> 00:44:36.390
where the firewall is deployed
behind the external load

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:39.090
balancer, and applying
all the security policies

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:41.220
that you would like
inbound and outbound.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:44.120
And the application stack
is deployed behind it.

00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:45.980
Now, as your load grows--

00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:46.650
oops.

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:50.220
Seems like I did not have
the animation picture here.

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:52.080
But essentially,
the idea would be

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:55.410
that when that application
stack scales out

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:58.140
horizontally and
adds more nodes,

00:44:58.140 --> 00:44:59.760
you can now also
add more firewall

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:03.720
nodes to meet with demand, based
on the metrics that are pushed

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:05.890
to Stackdriver, for example.

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:08.550
So that would give you
a scale-out architecture

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:12.420
to deal with load and
elasticity of the cloud.

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:17.040
So in general, that example that
I gave is the rightmost one.

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:19.560
This is about internet
gateway perimeter security,

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:20.670
north-south.

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:22.290
The two other most
common use cases

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:24.365
for protecting
deployments in the cloud,

00:45:24.365 --> 00:45:28.230
the leftmost one being hybrid
cloud, where a lot of customers

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:31.470
have on-prem networks that
are connecting into the Google

00:45:31.470 --> 00:45:33.390
Cloud environment, and
they want to make sure

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:38.450
that that is connecting
securely and that if any VM

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:40.550
in the Google side
is compromised,

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:44.300
malware does not come
down, or somebody on prem

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:46.492
is not exfiltrating
data up to the cloud.

00:45:46.492 --> 00:45:48.200
So you're trying to
make sure that you're

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:51.710
doing application-level security
and inspection for malware.

00:45:51.710 --> 00:45:55.430
And the one in the middle
is about segmentation, which

00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:57.640
is about making sure that--

00:45:57.640 --> 00:45:59.319
let's say you have
one project deployed

00:45:59.319 --> 00:46:00.860
which has a high
level of trust where

00:46:00.860 --> 00:46:03.710
all your intellectual property
and important assets live,

00:46:03.710 --> 00:46:06.320
and there's another project
which has lower level of trust.

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:08.300
You would like to
connect the two of them

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:09.950
so that there is
some data sharing,

00:46:09.950 --> 00:46:12.810
but you want to do deeper
inspection between them.

00:46:12.810 --> 00:46:16.760
So that's what a segmentation
architecture would look like.

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:17.609
That's about it.

00:46:17.609 --> 00:46:19.400
If you want to know
more, you can come down

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:21.680
to the booth to learn more.

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:23.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:26.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

